n the nutribullet  with  coconut oil and cacao and salt  oh i forgot the other thing in the  morning i do  half a lemon lukewarm water and a  quarter of a teaspoon of  pink salt because that just goes into  your adrenals and just  i can't make this up folks but i must  say that this  does feel like every wellness  influencers breakfast that i've ever  reviewed  all rolled into one and yet still  somehow it manages to be really low in  calories  it is impressive to consume so much  with so little actual fuel but anyways  we have a lot of ground to cover here so  ian claims that his smoothie situation  gives him the perfect  combination of electrolytes fat and  caffeine  which gives him a oh of energy as he  says  i don't know of his definition of that's  getting a little  glottal collateral is the same as mine  but i guess i just have to kind of like  take his word for it as true  what i can say is he is getting a lot of  hydration from the broth and tea and  green juice and lemon water  and he's choosing antioxidant and  vitamin rich options as well with the  tea and cacao and the juice  but honestly this meal here  reads kind of like a goop list of  anti-aging  superfoods to start i have talked about  lemon water on this channel ad nauseum  and i've also written an in-depth  article on the stuff as well so i will  link to that below  but to summarize drink it if you like it  as a flavorful way to get your water in  but no it is not going to detox you  de-blow to you cure your acne or lead to  weight loss unless you are  literally drinking it in place of food  the best thing that will come out of  drinking lemon water  is hydration and potentially kidney  stone prevention but  unless ian is prone to stones it's  probably  not a major perk for him as for ian's  comment about it going into your  adrenals  i guess this is in reference to the  wellness culture assumption that  lemon water while acidic is thought by  many to be  alkalinizing due to the fact that it  releases alkaline  byproducts when it's metabolized but  that has no  effect on your adrenals really because  food ph  doesn't even influence blood ph period  blood ph is very tightly regulated in  the body  otherwise we would die quite easily from  eating something  just slightly sour but also if anything  were to help our adrenals function more  optimally it would be  basic stress relief so if a warm cup of  lemon water calms your nerves  then great you have a natural adrenal  fatigue  cure though side note a systematic  review  did suggest that adrenal fatigue isn't  necessarily a real thing  adrenal insufficiency however is  recognized in the medical community  and one outcome of this aldosterone  deficiency is sometimes treated with  extra salt so maybe just maybe if ian is  managing an actual clinical condition  called aldosterone deficiency that his  lemon and salt water  may come in handy but otherwise it's  just a flavorful source of hydration  that was a lot now for bone broth i  mentioned this in my kylie jenner video  right here  but it is a rich source of collagen  protein and gelatin  and has very few calories about 50  calories per cup  it's not going to magically heal your  gut like so many proponents insist  but there is some evidence for gelatin's  role in rheumatoid arthritis  and because it is so high in protein it  will definitely help to fill you up  as for the coconut oil can we just put  that one to bed  coconut oil became really popular  because of its alleged fat burning  properties thanks to its mct or medium  chain triglyceride content  however while mcts are processed  differently than other fats in the body  and therefore they may have slight  metabolic advantages over other  saturated fats  unfortunately most coconut oil only  contains around  50 mcts and the mct  that it's most rich in lauric acid  doesn't appear to have as many of those  ketogenic fat burning benefits  compared to caprylic or capric acid  both of which together only make up  around 12 percent  of its fat as we're getting electrolytes  from himalayan sea salt  yes it does have greater amounts of some  beneficial electrolytes when compared to  table salt  so about three times the potassium 100  times the magnesium  and four times the calcium per gram but  the only mineral you'll get in  substantial quantities in salt  is sodium and there's more sodium in  table salt than that expensive stuff  if that is your goal so use whatever  salt that you like  and get your electrolytes like potassium  from a more abundant  and cheaper source like a banana tomato  or an orange  okay moving on  next ian says that he works out and then  he has another breakfast meal  this time thankfully with actual food  [Applause]  so he says he has two giant handfuls of  nuts  plus some kind of ocean fish or fresh  water fish  broiled or pan-fried in a cast iron  skillet or ceramic pan  but not non-stick in his words  nonstick is as toxic as the day is long  we'll come back to that in a hot minute  he then finishes things off with some  sunflower oil salt and lemon  and serves it with two handfuls of  vegetables  finally he finishes his meal with some  homemade chia pudding made with coconut  milk  chia salt and cacao oh boy  this is going to be a doozy  food burst misinformation first food  burst  misinformation let's  tackle the food first because i'm hungry  so this sounds like a really awesome  meal albeit pretty low carb  but it's quite the production  considering he says he does  all of this before the baby wakes at 8  30 a.m  i mean pretty impressive considering  this mom right here is like  eating toast scraps off of my kids plate  just like trying to survive  but anyway it is nutritious stuff so  we've got  loads of fiber from the veggies and the  chia  fats from the fish coconut milk nuts and  oil  and of course the quality protein in the  fish and this is a substantial size meal  which really helps to make up for the  liquid diet that he described earlier on  now trigger warning i am going to talk  calories for a minute but if he was to  eat all of this  we're looking at potentially up to  around 1600 calories  for this meal alone impressive  now first of all he specifies ocean or  freshwater fish  i assume he means over choosing farmed  fish  and that might be an environmentally  friendly choice  and it might not depends on the species  when we compare farmed versus wild  atlantic salmon for example  we see that farmed salmon has three  times the saturated fat  25 percent more omega-3s and twice the  calories  as wild some wild fish contain higher  levels of mercury  due to bioaccumulation but farm fish  tend to contain  higher levels of other contaminants such  as dioxins  and pcbs and also higher amounts of  antibiotics  as for sustainability some of both wild  and farm seafood is considered  unsustainable  and some is considered restorative so it  would really  heavily depend on the unique species how  it was caught  and where it was caught so when you're  in doubt you're trying to look for a  sustainable choice  run your choice through seafoodwatch.org  to see their recommendations it is an  amazing amazing resource so i'm going to  link  that below okay next let's talk about  the sunflower oil that he mentions which  actually comes in four different  varieties each with relative  amounts of different fatty acids i don't  know which one  ian uses but i will say that the high  oleic variety  is higher in monounsaturated fats and  lower in omega-6  polyunsaturated fats that have been  linked to inflammation when consumed in  excess  so if you're going for sunflower oil a  high oleic variety  is definitely considered the best for  your heart  and then there is the nonstick cookware  is toxic statement  okay first of all i love myself a good  cast iron skillet myself and i do  believe  it's superior to non-stick but i still  want to debunk this myth a bit because  ian's statement felt unnecessarily  fear-mongering  i actually wrote a whole blog post about  this that i will link below  but i think this concern comes out of  the traditional use of a compound called  pfoa and nonstick cookware  which was linked to kidney and  testicular cancer in one study  however not only do we have no evidence  suggesting that using  non-stick cookware specifically can lead  to these cancers  nonstick cookware has been pfoa since  around 2013.  so that one is not really a concern  anymore  unless you haven't replaced your pan now  the teflon coating found on non-stick  cookware today  is typically harmless until it reaches  very high temperatures  so at extremely high temperatures of  around 500 degrees fahrenheit  teflon will begin to break down causing  the release of  two toxic chemicals tetrafluoromethane  and carbon and inhaling these fumes may  potentially result in what is  affectionately known as  the teflon flu in early animal studies  exposure has led to symptoms like  headaches chills  backaches and a fever beginning about  four to 10 hours after  exposure and then resolving again around  12 to 48 hours after exposure  these risks were only evident when the  teflon cookware was heated to  temperatures  over 570 degrees fahrenheit so it's  important to note  that we don't have any human research as  of yet  to determine the relative human risk  also  those crazy high temperatures are rarely  reached when we cook  since most fats and butters and oils  start to smoke around 400 degrees  fahrenheit in more severe cases where  the cookware was heated beyond 660  degrees fahrenheit for an extended  period of time  research suggests the teflon cookware  may increase the risk of respiratory  problems including shortness of breath  and acute pulmonary edema so  should you can your non-stick not if  it's being purchased after 2013  you can simply use your non-stick for  low or medium heat  avoid heating an empty pan so that is  use a little bit of oil or butter to  protect that pan  make sure that you have ventilation in  your kitchen while cooking  and again replace any really old  nonstick cookware from before 2013  that has pfoa ultimately i say new pots  and pans are always a great holiday gift  for the food lover in your life so  maybe you put that on your list for  santa okay so moving on  ian mentions that twice a week he has a  massive  stack of pancakes which really doesn't  surprise me given how restrictive and  low-carb his usual morning routine is  eating his diet i would probably also go  overboard on the carbs when i had a  chance to  but anyways he says that all of that  happens before the baby gets up  around 8 30 a.m and then  we're not done folks he gets to sit and  what does he have  more steamed vegetables because i'm sure  that  is what i would be craving to if i had  only eaten tea and broth and vegetables  and fish before  9 a.m but when it's time to feed the  baby we finally get to see  some variety in play so he describes  having fruit  sheep's milk or coconut yogurt and hash  browns and i assume  not the iconic mcdonald's variety and  side note  like this video if you think the hash  browns are the best thing on the  mcdonald's menu  because i definitely do okay this  definitely seems like a good breakfast  on its own  we've got fiber from the fruit and the  potato hash browns  and some fat and protein from the yogurt  i will say that coconut yogurt  while vegan is really low in protein  like we're talking  1 gram versus 10 grams for sheep's milk  yogurt so  if he does eat animal products the  sheep's milk yogurt might be a better  pick  also i assume he specified sheep's milk  yogurt because of its alleged digestive  benefits  so i definitely want to unpack that  while research specifically comparing  fresh sheet milk to cow's milk is  lacking  there is some evidence on the size of  fat globules and digestion of goat's  milk formulas for babies  now from this we know that the fat  globules of goat's milk  are smaller than that of cow's milk and  some scientists have speculated that  these smaller fat globules  might make it easier for our bodies to  break down but considering that breast  milk globules are even bigger than cow's  milk  it doesn't really seem to make for a  very good case that this would play a  major role  in digestibility difference as for  lactose  cow's milk contains around 4.93 lactose  sheep's milk is 4.76 and goats is 4.33  so if lactose is an issue for you you're  better off choosing  goat's milk dairy than sheep sheep's  milk dairy  is also a premium ticket item since the  average sheep produces  only a half a gallon of milk per day  compared to cows producing  around six to eight so if you like and  can access  sheep's milk products great amazing  they are obviously super nutritious and  for some  they might make a measurable difference  in digestibility  but for most people they're not  necessarily going to  make or break your ability to enjoy some  dairy in your day  let's see what he has to say about his  meal  but when you eat really clean you're  eating  tremendous amounts most of the stuff we  get is  farmers market stuff or we grow it this  is  feeling a lot like that sorella moore  video that i reviewed right here  and that he definitely has a strong  elitist food snob vibe  going on yeah so let's first discuss  ian's suggestion that he grows most of  his food or buys from the farmers market  sure the idea of growing our own food is  so romantic  and probably a really beautiful healthy  way to live  no question about that but it's not  realistic for  99.9999999 percent of people who are  watching  who don't have the time or money or land  or other resources  to harvest their own kale and raise  their own sheep to make  milk and then ferment into yogurt as for  ian's comment that  when you eat clean you end up eating a  tremendous amount  yes nutrient-dense whole foods are  typically lower in calories than  processed foods so  for example calorie for calorie if you  were to have a meal for mcdonald's  with a big mac and medium fries and a  medium drink  you're looking at around 1100 calories  to eat that quantity in the kind of  foods that ian enjoys  you'd be looking at a meal of four cups  of kale  eight ounces of salmon two and a half  cups of potatoes  a cup of sheep's milk yogurt and one and  a half cups of  berries for most people that would be  a pretty large and very satiating meal  much more so than the burger fries and  coke and that's kind of a good thing for  most people  but if ian was finding it hard to meet  his needs eating whole foods  he could add more higher calorie higher  fat foods  like cooking with more oil adding some  avocados nuts  nut butters and seeds okay so we are  done  with breakfast let's talk about lunch  basically it's a lot of the same same  deal  ian says he has more fish huge amounts  of vegetables  he has sweet potatoes quinoa regular  potatoes and  high density fats i'm not totally sure  what he means by high density  i assume he's not talking about hdl  which is high density lipoprotein  aka they good cholesterol in our blood  which  is not something that you can like just  pour on a salad  but if he means calorically dense well  all oils and fats are pretty calorie  dense with somewhere around 100 calories  per tablespoon  so i'd say choose an oil or fat that  works for the job  and that you enjoy and use it in  moderation  next of course is more lemon water which  we already covered  and that's followed by his other  favorite drink or coffee  i do do a lot of coffee and tea but i  also mix it with a tablespoon of coconut  oil  and if you whip it you're binding  caffeine  molecules to a fat lipid so your body  burns it  slower and more evenly as opposed to  like straight up and straight down  uh so okay ian has more coffee in the  afternoon apparently this is his vice  but he says that if you whip it with  coconut oil  you'll get a slower absorption compared  to a massive caffeine rush  so this was an interesting statement and  it's not untrue  though i think he is making it a lot  more complicated than needs be  basically we do have research to suggest  that having caffeine on an empty stomach  will increase blood saturation rate  whereas consuming caffeine  like anything with fat will slow stomach  emptying reducing the spike in caffeine  hit and likely contributing to a more  even but potentially longer buzz  and while whipping or emulsifying is  used for other drugs to modulate  absorption in half light  you'll likely see the same benefits just  from drinking coffee  with some food particularly something  that is rich in fiber  protein or fat it's pretty simple  but anyways let's move on yes sugar is  the devil  one-fifth of all the sugar and one-fifth  of all the oxygen your body intakes your  brain uses  so you have to remember to give it that  i always deal with the rock says  it's not a cheap it's a treat meal okay  so before i talk about ian's  treat meal let's unpack this statement  first of all i'm not sure about the stat  that ian cites suggesting that our brain  uses one-fifth of the sugar  and one-fifth of the oxygen in our body  but i can say  that even though the brain and body can  absolutely function without carbs  that is literally the premise of keto  the brain does enjoy  a good carb so i do agree with him there  where i don't agree  is calling sugar the devil obviously  straight up sugar is not the most  nutritious substance on the planet  but we can make foods morally equal even  if they're not  nutritionally equal and sugar is just  another tool in the chef's toolbox  it's something that can be enjoyed in  moderation  and used to make other nutrient dense  foods more exciting  as for ian's treat meal a name that i  actually much prefer to the ubiquitous  cheat meal he says that he has a giant  stack of gluten-free pancakes with fruit  or maple syrup  now first of all of course they got to  be gluten free  but a reminder to the people in the back  that unless you have celiac or a gluten  intolerance  there's no reason to be choosing  gluten-free products  in fact a lot of gluten-free grains are  lower in fiber and less nutritious than  their gluten-fried  counterparts but anyway ian also says  that because maple syrup requires a lot  of energy to produce  he instead suggests using honey or  making a homemade blueberry compote  sustainability is such a complicated  subject with so much to consider  so what you choose to pour in your  pancakes is really such a personal  choice  now i will say that it is also true that  it takes 40 gallons of sap to make  one gallon of syrup but while this may  sound like  a lot well-managed maple trees can be  a really sustainable option these trees  tend to produce sap for  decades and when they do actually die  naturally  they can then be used to fuel  evaporators that boil the syrup  ideally though of course to keep the  carbon footprint to a minimum  it's best to choose maple syrup from a  local source wherever possible  and thankfully being canadian that's an  easy choice  for me now what other treats does ian  allow himself on his  treat day well it wouldn't be a  superfood listicle without a mention of  dark chocolate which he says he chooses  both for the antioxidants  and the caffeine again i don't know  this guy must just not sleep now for  dinner  ian says he focuses on super crazy  healthy starches  potatoes sweet potatoes quinoa  gluten-free pasta  and then serves that with vegetables and  fish  yawn i mean  this guy's diet is certainly nutritious  but it is  boring as f i am not sure i could eat  that volume of food  every day if i was limited to such a  narrow boring set of foods  over and over and over he does however  describe occasionally making a vegan  meal so  i'm excited about that let's see how he  switches things up you know you can take  one big head of lettuce cut it up put it  in a cast iron pan  water and salt cook it down a bit boom  olive oil  spices whatever you want and bam you  have this like amazing  vegan amazing meal you can put it over  rice quinoa okay um  can we just get something straight  lettuce  like cooked or raw is not a good vegan  meal it's like when you go to a wedding  and check off the vegan entree option  and you are literally just given a plate  of whatever side vegetables  everyone else is getting with their meat  i mean  at least things like carrots and  potatoes would yield some trace protein  and fiber but  lettuce not so much i'm freaking  hungry i mean serve that lettuce with  some tofu or chickpeas or lentils or  something else but all right so let's  wrap this up because somehow i probably  turned a five minute video into like a  20 minute  review but obviously i like a vampire  just as much as any millennial girl  let's be real  but even vampires eat more exciting  diets than this  obviously this is not a dig at ian's  professional life  or his health status or really anything  else about him  he obviously takes really good care of  his body  and he eats an incredibly healthy  nutrient-dense  diet i just feel that this video alone  perpetuates  a lot of food fear and is a perfect  demonstration  of a pretty elitist privileged  relationship with food  obviously celebrities have access to  money  time and other resources that most of us  just do not have  so we can't expect to do everything like  them  but man this diet is just so  far from relatable you would almost  think it was a parody  there are so many competing values a lot  of us have to balance  when it comes to what to eat whether  that's price availability sustainability  ethics allergies family preferences and  more  there should be no shame or guilt if  your choices  are less than ideal for one or more of  these considerations  but friends this is where variety comes  into play  if you are eating a variety of foods not  only are you ensuring your body is  getting  a wide range of nutrients without really  tracking or thinking about it  but you're also diluting the  environmental impact  on any one natural resource so if i were  to give  ian any feedback on his diet not even  touching on his relationship with food  because that would take  a lot to unpack i would say that variety  is the spice of life  and there is a lot more out there than  kale lemon water coconut oil and fish  and on that note thank you again to  squarespace for sponsoring this video  if you enjoyed this video and you want  to see me do more of these harper bazaar  reviews  be sure to like this video and leave me  a comment below on who i should check  out next  until then don't forget to subscribe to  the channel ring that little bell so you  never miss out  and i will see you next time on abby's  kitchen bye  you

